How to Write Your Own Rule
**************************

.[perex]
From writing your first test to running a custom rule across your codebase in a single afternoon: define the decision your rule enforces, specify the syntax tree nodes you want to inspect, write your conditions alongside the automated fix, and let `RuleTester` handle everything else.


What We Are Going to Write
==========================

Every team has a few bespoke coding conventions that no standard covers because they belong to that team alone. Take this common example: an exception message is a complete sentence and must end with a period. A statement like `throw new InvalidArgumentException('The name must not be empty')` should therefore automatically become `'The name must not be empty.'`. For years, I watched for this manually in code reviews because writing a custom sniff for PHP_CodeSniffer was more trouble than it was worth. In DressCode, we can implement this cleanly in about fifty lines, and we'll write them together.


The Fixture First
=================

Every rule in DressCode is developed and tested against fixtures: paired files containing code before and after an automated fix. One fixture has up to three files: `.code` containing the input code, `.expected` containing the expected output after the fix, and `.violations` listing the exact line numbers and messages the rule must report. A fixture is the most unambiguous specification you can write, so we begin right there in `tests/fixtures/exceptionMessagePeriod/basic.code`:

```php
<?php

throw new InvalidArgumentException('The name must not be empty');
throw new \RuntimeException('Cannot connect to the database.');
throw new App\NotFoundException("Order $id not found");
throw new LogicException('Unreachable ');
$logger->log('Started');
```

Right here, we define the exact boundaries of what our rule should and shouldn't do: the second line is already valid; the third contains an interpolated string that we intentionally leave untouched (we can't predict what `$id` will evaluate to); the fourth has trailing whitespace that the period should absorb; and the fifth isn't an exception at all. The `basic.expected` file contains the exact same code with periods added on lines 3 and 6, while `basic.violations` specifies the expected reports:

```
3: The message of an exception must end with a period.
6: The message of an exception must end with a period.
```


The Skeleton of the Rule
========================

A rule is a class extending `DressCode\NodeRule`, decorated with the `#[RuleInfo]` attribute specifying which execution stage it belongs to. There are three stages (`Stage`): `Structure` for structural code changes, `Formatting` for whitespace and line breaks, and `Finishing` for final cleanup. Because our rule modifies text content, it belongs in `Structure`.

A rule has no public name. In DressCode, users never configure or enable rules directly: they configure **decisions** (configuration keys that define how code should look), and rules merely enforce them. Our decision is that exception messages must end with a period. The rule declares this via `getDecisions()`:

```php
namespace App\CodeStyle;

use DressCode\Decision;
use DressCode\Domain;
use DressCode\NodeRule;
use DressCode\RuleContext;
use DressCode\RuleInfo;
use DressCode\Stage;
use PhpSyntax\Node;
use PhpSyntax\Nodes\Expression\NewNode;
use PhpSyntax\Token;


#[RuleInfo(Stage::Structure)]
final class ExceptionMessagePeriodRule extends NodeRule
{
	public static function getDecisions(): array
	{
		return [
			new Decision('project.exceptionMessagePeriod', Domain::state('required'), 'The message of an exception ends with a period'),
		];
	}


	public function getVisitedNodes(): array
	{
		return [NewNode::class];
	}


	public function enter(Node|Token $node, RuleContext $context): void
	{
	}
}
```

A `Decision` consists of three parts. The **path** `project.exceptionMessagePeriod` connects everything the user sees: they write it in their configuration, see it next to every finding in the terminal, use it in `dresscode:ignore` comments, and query it using `dresscode explain`. Project-specific rules operate in the `project` section, plugin rules use a section named after the plugin, and the engine uses its twenty or so built-in sections, ensuring paths never collide. The **domain** `Domain::state('required')` defines accepted values: the string `required` ("periods are mandatory") and, like every decision that requires something, `keep` ("do not check"). Finally, the **description** is an English sentence without a trailing period that explains what the rule does where the key is not `keep`; it is displayed by `dresscode explain` and in the generated catalogue.

A decision like this is called a **requirement**: the rule runs wherever any configuration layer sets it to a value other than `keep`. Where no configuration mentions it, it requires nothing and does not run. Rules are therefore never enabled by class name: a project simply declares its desired code style, and the corresponding rule joins the run automatically.

`NodeRule` is one of two rule types: it traverses syntax tree nodes and inspects them directly. The other type is `GapRule`, which doesn't visit nodes, but [submits declarative claims about whitespace |whitespace-rules] instead.

The `getVisitedNodes()` method declares which node types you want to inspect. The engine will call `enter()` exclusively for those nodes. We care about instantiation via `new`, represented by `NewNode`. The [node reference |phpsyntax:nodes] lists all available nodes and their slots: `NewNode` provides `newKeyword`, `class`, and `arguments`.


Conditions and the Fix
======================

Now for the core logic. In `enter()`, we evaluate a series of conditions against the syntax tree to detect violations (adding imports for `PhpSyntax\Nodes\NameNode` and `PhpSyntax\Nodes\Scalar\StringNode`):

```php
public function enter(Node|Token $node, RuleContext $context): void
{
	if (
		!$node instanceof NewNode
		|| !$node->class instanceof NameNode
		|| !str_ends_with($node->class->text, 'Exception')
	) {
		return;
	}

	$message = $node->arguments?->items->getItems()[0]->value ?? null;
	if (
		!$message instanceof StringNode
		|| $message->quote !== "'"
		|| $message->toValue() === ''
		|| str_ends_with(rtrim($message->toValue()), '.')
		|| !$context->report($message, 'The message of an exception must end with a period.')
	) {
		return;
	}

	$message->setValue(rtrim($message->toValue()) . '.');
}
```

Read the checks from the top down: this is a `new` instantiation with an explicit class name (not `new $class` or an anonymous class), and the class name ends with `Exception`. The first argument is a plain string without interpolation: an interpolated string in double quotes produces a completely different node, so the third line of our fixture is filtered out automatically without any special conditions. That string uses single quotes, is non-empty, and its trimmed value doesn't already end with a period.

And the crucial final check: **nobody suppressed the decision at this location.** The `$context->report()` method returns `false` if the construct is [suppressed |suppressions-and-baseline] by a `dresscode:ignore` comment on the same line or above it, or by a `dresscode:disable`/`dresscode:enable` block. In that scenario, the code must not change. That is why `report()` sits at the end of the `if` condition rather than before it. You don't need to pass the decision path manually: a rule with a single requirement reports under it automatically.

Only after passing all these guards do we apply the fix: a single call to `setValue()`. We don't overwrite raw token text; we update the string's semantic value, letting the node handle quotes and escaping automatically. The rest of the file remains byte-for-byte identical.

Notice what this rule never has to deal with: no parenthesis counting, no hunting for argument boundaries, and no special handling for inline comments nested inside the call. The syntax tree handles all of that. On a flat array of tokens, most of your code would be spent defending against things that can appear between `new` and the string literal; in the tree, that problem never arises.


Testing the Rule
================

Fixtures need to declare what value the decision runs with; otherwise it defaults to `keep` and the rule does nothing. Configuration values for all fixtures in a directory belong in a `values.neon` file placed alongside them, matching standard configuration syntax:

```neon
project:
	exceptionMessagePeriod: required
```

The `RuleTester` utility takes the rule class and the fixtures directory, automatically verifying everything you might otherwise forget to test: that the output matches `.expected`, that reported findings match `.violations`, that running the rule on its own output mutates nothing further (idempotence), that comments are fully preserved, that `dresscode:ignoreFile` is respected, and that nothing was modified without being reported. In Nette Tester, a test looks like this:

```php
use App\CodeStyle\ExceptionMessagePeriodRule;
use DressCode\Testing\RuleTester;
use Tester\Assert;

require __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';
Tester\Environment::setup();

Assert::noError(fn() => RuleTester::run(ExceptionMessagePeriodRule::class, __DIR__ . '/fixtures/exceptionMessagePeriod'));
```

Nette Tester expects at least one assertion per test, which is why the call is wrapped in `Assert::noError()`. In PHPUnit, you would place the exact same call inside a test method. Any assertion failure throws a `DressCode\Testing\TestFailure` exception with a unified diff, highlighting exactly where the output diverged from expectations.

Run the test now, and it will pass. If it fails, the diff reveals the exact line where the rule sees code differently than you do. That is often when you discover subtle edge cases: in our case, `rtrim()` was missing in the first draft, and the fixture with trailing whitespace immediately prompted its addition.


Enabling It in a Project
========================

You can place your rule class anywhere your autoloader can find it. A good convention is placing custom rules in `tools/CodeStyle/` loaded via `autoload-dev`, preventing `DressCode\NodeRule` from entering your production autoloader. In your configuration, register the rule class under `rules`, which adds its decisions to the catalogue. Then configure its value in the `project` section, just like any built-in decision:

```neon
rules:
	- App\CodeStyle\ExceptionMessagePeriodRule

project:
	exceptionMessagePeriod: required
```

From this point on, `dresscode check` reports violations under its full path:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/check.php
  error  8:39  The message of an exception must end with a period.  project.exceptionMessagePeriod
\--

The `dresscode fix` command repairs it automatically, `// dresscode:ignore project.exceptionMessagePeriod` suppresses it locally, and `dresscode explain project.exceptionMessagePeriod` displays its description, domain, and configuring layer, exactly like any built-in engine decision. When multiple projects need the same rule, wrap it in a [plugin |presets-and-plugins]; its decisions then simply relocate to the plugin's dedicated section.


Where to Go Next
================

- When a rule needs additional values (such as a configurable list of exception name suffixes), it declares a **parameter** alongside its requirement: a companion decision that refines behavior and is read in `configure()`. See [A Rule in Detail |rule-contract] for how to do it, the domains, and everything a rule must follow.
- Our rule identifies exceptions simply by checking if their name ends with `Exception`. If you need more than that, use the `NameResolver` analysis to resolve names against imports and namespaces; available analyses are documented in [PhpSyntax |phpsyntax:analyses].
- Rules controlling spaces, indentation, and line breaks are written differently, submitting declarative claims instead of implementing `enter()`: [Whitespace Rules |whitespace-rules].
- If you are migrating an existing sniff from CodeSniffer or a fixer from PHP CS Fixer, check our porting guide: [Porting a Rule from Another Tool |porting-rules].

